-- The bar: "an appeal cannot be brought against any decision of a Judge in the action except in the circumstances
-- set out in that sub-paragraph".  The circumstances are in the Supreme Court of Judicature Act (not among the
-- sources): an input, NOTHING when the asker does not supply it.
-- COVERS O 46A r 1(5)
@ref O 46A r 1(5) (src:6134)
GIVEN `the action is on the Express Track` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the decision is a decision of a Judge in the action` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the circumstances set out in paragraph 4(1) of the Fourth Schedule to the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 exist` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 46A r 1(5) an appeal is barred under this paragraph` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the action is on the Express Track`)
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an action is on the Express Track" is a "where" condition; the bar is not engaged
        IF NOT (`the decision is a decision of a Judge in the action`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar is on appeals "against any decision of a Judge in the action"; another decision is outside its scope
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the circumstances set out in paragraph 4(1) of the Fourth Schedule to the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 exist`
            WHEN JUST exist THEN
                IF exist
                THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except in the circumstances set out in that sub-paragraph" lifts the bar
                ELSE JUST TRUE
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the circumstances are set out in paragraph 4(1) of the Fourth Schedule to the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969, which is not among the sources

-- The permission question (QA).  "an appeal cannot be brought": JUST FALSE while the bar holds.  Where the
-- exception is met the bar is lifted, and the paragraph grants nothing: NOTHING.  Its yes/no twin is the function above.
-- COVERS O 46A r 1(5)
-- JUDGMENT QA | O 46A r 1(5) | `o 46A r 1(5) an appeal can be brought against the decision under this paragraph` | NOTHING when the exception is met (a lifted bar grants nothing); the twin `o 46A r 1(5) an appeal is barred under this paragraph` answers JUST FALSE | JUST TRUE (the exception permits it)
@ref O 46A r 1(5) (src:6134)
GIVEN `the action is on the Express Track` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the decision is a decision of a Judge in the action` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the circumstances set out in paragraph 4(1) of the Fourth Schedule to the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 exist` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 46A r 1(5) an appeal can be brought against the decision under this paragraph` MEANS
    CONSIDER `o 46A r 1(5) an appeal is barred under this paragraph` `the action is on the Express Track` `the decision is a decision of a Judge in the action` `the circumstances set out in paragraph 4(1) of the Fourth Schedule to the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 exist`
    WHEN JUST barred THEN
        IF barred
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an appeal cannot be brought against any decision of a Judge in the action"
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the exception lifts the bar and grants nothing; whether an appeal lies is for the Supreme Court of Judicature Act
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): see `o 46A r 1(5) an appeal is barred under this paragraph`

